{"answer":"The reserve that receives deposited excess fish is managed by a rotating committee of fishermen, chaired by the community watcher.  Each deposit is recorded in the shared ledger as a separate ‘deposited’ entry: the exact weight, species (if identifiable), the fisher’s name, the date, and a note that the fish were placed in the reserve.  The community watcher signs that entry and, after the fish are physically placed in the reserve tank, updates a separate reserve inventory log that tracks the cumulative stock and any future removal for restoration projects.  This dual record keeps the reserve’s holdings transparent and ties each deposit back to the fisher’s compliance record.

Restated policy (unchanged):
"Each fisher may take up to 10 kg per trip and must leave the remaining catch for community conservation. Fishermen record their catch on a shared ledger before setting out. If a fisher exceeds 10 kg, the excess must be returned to the lake or deposited in a community reserve that funds restoration projects. A designated community watcher will verify that the excess is returned or deposited and record that action in the ledger. Every two weeks we meet to review the ledger, discuss any breaches, and adjust the quota if overall lake levels rise or fall. Non‑compliance results in a one‑trip suspension from fishing privileges. 1. Before the trip, the fisher records the planned catch on the shared ledger. 2. If the catch exceeds 10 kg, the fisher drops the excess fish back into the lake or takes them to the community reserve. 3. The community watcher observes this action, writes a note in the ledger marking it as ‘returned’ or ‘deposited’, and signs the entry. 4. The fisher also signs the ledger to confirm the record. 5. The ledger is kept in a communal box and reviewed at the bi‑weekly meeting to track compliance."","revised_policy":"Each fisher may take up to 10 kg per trip and must leave the remaining catch for community conservation. Fishermen record their catch on a shared ledger before setting out. If a fisher exceeds 10 kg, the excess must be returned to the lake or deposited in a community reserve that funds restoration projects. A designated community watcher will verify that the excess is returned or deposited and record that action in the ledger. Every two weeks we meet to review the ledger, discuss any breaches, and adjust the quota if overall lake levels rise or fall. Non‑compliance results in a one‑trip suspension from fishing privileges. 1. Before the trip, the fisher records the planned catch on the shared ledger. 2. If the catch exceeds 10 kg, the fisher drops the excess fish back into the lake or takes them to the community reserve. 3. The community wat